# <%= @name %>

A Phoenix 1.8 and Ash 3 application generated by bubble_ex from a Bubble
app's data model.

## Getting started

    mix setup              # deps, database, assets
    mix ash.codegen initial  # migrations for the generated resources
    mix ash.migrate
    mix phx.server         # http://localhost:4000

`mix test` runs the smoke tests (the endpoint boots, magic-link sign-in).
It creates and migrates its own database: `TEST_DATABASE_URL` when set,
otherwise `<%= @app %>_test` on `localhost` (`config/test.exs`); never
`DATABASE_URL`, which may point at a database you care about.
`mix wtf.task complete` and `audit` run tests only on a database you name
(`--test-db URL`, or `--use-project-test-config`; see `mix help
wtf.task`).

## Generated and owned files

`.wtf/generated.json` lists the **generated** files with their SHA-256.
They are derived from the Bubble app (the Ash resources, enums and types,
the token resource, `Accounts.Resources`, the workflow API entry point, the
file serving (`<%= @web %>.Uploads`, its controller and test), the
Tailwind theme tokens in `assets/css/bubble.css` and the name map
`.wtf/names.json`). Regeneration overwrites them, so do not edit them
by hand: a hand edit is detected and blocks regeneration. Change the
Bubble app or record an owner decision instead.

Every other file (this README, `mix.exs`, the config, the router, layouts,
controllers, the magic-link sender, tests) is **owned**: scaffolded once,
then yours. Regeneration never overwrites it.

## Data model

- Every record keeps its Bubble `_id` as a writable string primary key `id`,
  so imports are upserts on the Bubble IDs.
- A reference to one thing is a `belongs_to` without a database foreign key;
  a list of things is an ordered `{:array, :string}` of Bubble IDs.
- Option sets are `Ash.Type.Enum` modules keyed by Bubble's stable option keys.
- Names are locked in `.wtf/names.json` at first generation, so later caption
  edits in Bubble do not rename code.

## Authorization: none yet

The resources are generated with `privacy: :omit`: they have **no
authorization**. Bubble's privacy rules are not enforced, so any code path
that reads a resource sees every record. Add Ash policies before exposing
any resource through an API, a LiveView or a controller.

## Migrated files

The data loader copies the Bubble app's files into a storage directory.
`/uploads/<sha256>/<name>` serves them (`<%= @web %>.UploadsController`;
set `UPLOADS_ROOT` to the loader's storage root and `UPLOADS_PUBLIC_URL`
to its `public_url`). A Bubble file field can hold another app's file,
so every file is treated as hostile: it downloads as an attachment of an
inert type (`application/octet-stream`, or `application/pdf`), except
PNG, JPEG, GIF and WebP images recognized by their bytes, which display
inline; responses carry `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and
`Content-Security-Policy: sandbox; default-src 'none'`. Pages link file
and image fields through `<%= @web %>.Uploads.url/1`.

**In production, serve public files from a separate origin:** set
`UPLOADS_HOST` to a separate domain (ideally not a subdomain of the app's)
that points at this app or a CDN in front of it; links use it, and file
requests on the app's own host redirect there. It must be `https://` and a
host (the app refuses to boot otherwise). On that host only public files
are served (`<%= @web %>.UploadsHostGuard`, first in the endpoint); the
app compares it with the request's `Host`, so a proxy or CDN must forward
the original `Host` header.

**Private files are off** (404, no links): the resources have no
authorization, and nothing yet says which record a private file belongs
to, so Bubble's "view attached files" rule is not enforced. To serve
them, replace `private: false` in `config/runtime.exs` with
`private: {MyModule, :authorize}` (your function gets the user,
or nil, and the file's `%{sha256, name}` and must return `true`), or
`private: :signed_in`, which lets **any** signed-in user who has a link
read the file: weaker than Bubble.

## Sign-in

Users sign in with a magic link (AshAuthentication). No password material
is read from Bubble, and only stored users can sign in. Emails match
case-insensitively (the User email is a trimmed `:ci_string`, unique).
`<%= @module %>.Accounts.UserAuthentication` (owned) holds the
authentication configuration; tune it there.
`<%= @module %>.Accounts.MagicLinkSender` enqueues an Oban job (unique per
email for a minute) that emails the link, encrypted in the job's
arguments. Throttle requests per IP at your proxy if you need to.

The User's `confirmed_at` (AshAuthentication's confirmation attribute)
says when the email was confirmed. Bubble keeps only a confirmed flag, so
users loaded from Bubble with a confirmed email have their Created Date
there, a migrated value; the others have nil. Signing in with a magic link
proves the email: `AuthController.success/4` sets `confirmed_at` when it
is nil.

## Configuration

Production reads its secrets from the environment and refuses to boot
without them: `DATABASE_URL`, `SECRET_KEY_BASE`, `TOKEN_SIGNING_SECRET`
(generate both with `mix phx.gen.secret`), `MAILER_ADAPTER` (a Swoosh
adapter such as `Swoosh.Adapters.Postmark`), `MAILER_FROM`, and the
adapter's `MAILER_API_KEY` / `MAILER_DOMAIN`. Optional: `PHX_HOST`, `PORT`,
`POOL_SIZE`.
